---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { REPO } from '../consts';

const title = 'Privacy Policy · Udaan';
const description = 'How Udaan handles data: local-first storage, bring-your-own AI provider keys, optional Gmail read-only sync, and what the landing site collects.';
const lastUpdated = 'September 17, 2026';
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pt-16 pb-24">
    <p class="font-mono text-xs tracking-[0.2em] text-ink-muted uppercase">Legal</p>
    <h1 class="mt-5 font-display text-4xl tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-5xl">Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-ink-muted">Last updated {lastUpdated}</p>

    <div class="prose mt-8">
      <p>
        Udaan is a local-first desktop application. The short version: your resume data lives in a
        SQLite database on your own device, there is no Udaan account or server that stores it, and
        anything sent to a third party (an AI provider, Google) is sent because you configured it and
        goes directly from your device — it does not pass through a server we operate, with the one
        exception noted in section 4.
      </p>

      <h2>1. What we don't have</h2>
      <p>
        There is no sign-up, no login, and no Udaan-operated database of user accounts or resumes.
        Nothing about your profile, job applications, or generated documents is transmitted to us. We
        have no analytics, crash reporting, or telemetry inside the desktop app itself (the marketing
        site you're reading this on is different — see section 6).
      </p>

      <h2>2. Where your data lives</h2>
      <p>
        Your profile, jobs, generated resumes and cover letters are stored in a local SQLite database
        on your device (<code>$APPDATA/app.db</code> in the desktop app). AI provider API keys are
        encrypted (AES-256-GCM) with a per-install key held in your OS keychain before being written to
        disk, or kept in browser <code>localStorage</code> if you're using the web build — never in the
        database, and never uploaded anywhere by us. You can export a full backup to a JSON file, or
        delete your data, at any time from Settings.
      </p>

      <h2>3. AI providers (bring your own key)</h2>
      <p>
        Tailoring a resume or cover letter sends the relevant text — your profile and the job
        description you pasted — directly from your device to the AI provider you chose and configured
        (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google Gemini, xAI Grok, Groq, DeepSeek, Mistral, OpenRouter, Perplexity, or
        a local Ollama instance that never leaves your machine). That request uses your own API key and
        is governed by that provider's own privacy policy and terms, not ours. We don't see, log, or
        retain the content of these requests.
      </p>

      <h2>4. The managed provider (optional)</h2>
      <p>
        If you opt into the pay-as-you-go managed provider instead of your own API key, your requests
        route through a LiteLLM gateway we operate, which forwards them to the upstream AI provider —
        this is the one path where a server we run is in the loop, solely to proxy the request and meter
        usage against your prepaid credits. Payment for credits is handled entirely by Stripe; we do not
        receive or store your card details.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Gmail integration (optional)</h2>
      <p>
        The email job tracker is off unless you turn it on and connect a Gmail account. It requests
        read-only access (<code>gmail.readonly</code>) to find and classify recruiting-related emails so
        they can be linked to your tracked applications.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Your Gmail OAuth tokens are stored encrypted on your device the same way API keys are —
          never in the database, never sent to a server we operate.</li>
        <li>Fetching messages, classifying them, and matching them to a job all happen on your device
          using the AI provider you've configured (see section 3) — Google's servers are the only
          third party in this flow.</li>
        <li>Synced emails (sender, subject, a snippet, and the classification result) are stored in your
          local database like everything else, and only ever leave your device if you export a backup.</li>
        <li>You can disconnect at any time from Settings, which stops the sync and clears the stored
          tokens; you can also revoke access directly from your
          <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google Account permissions</a>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Udaan's use and transfer of information received from Google APIs adheres to the
        <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google API Services User Data Policy</a>,
        including the Limited Use requirements: this data is used only to power the email tracking
        feature described above, is not sold, and is not used for advertising.
      </p>

      <h2>6. This website</h2>
      <p>
        The marketing site you're reading now (not the desktop app) uses Cloudflare Web Analytics for
        pageviews and Core Web Vitals — no cookies, no cross-site tracking, and no personally
        identifiable information collected. If you sign up for the mailing list or RSS feed, we use that
        address only to send what you asked for.
      </p>

      <h2>7. Children's privacy</h2>
      <p>
        Udaan is not directed at children under 13, and we do not knowingly collect personal
        information from them — largely moot given the desktop app collects nothing to begin with.
      </p>

      <h2>8. Changes to this policy</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes in a way that matters, we'll update the date at the top and note it in
        the <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">changelog</a>.
        Since Udaan is open source, you can also read the code yourself to verify any of the above.
      </p>

      <h2>9. Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about this policy: open an issue on
        <a href={`${REPO}/issues`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
